{:edb/db-before-t 165 :edb/db-after-t 166 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533478 50 #inst "2026-10-11T02:44:18.429Z" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186054611 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399610 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059691 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399489 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059691 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399496 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059772 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399603 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060369 1068 #edb/ref 17592186058806 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186060369 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399544 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060369 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060369 1074 "![](images/02/0204cfb247273efc20a31366dcb84096d630aa6dbe90c94a8802834205bbdd56.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=sin⁡x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-sin⁡x.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=-sin⁡x.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064291 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399375 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064894 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063390 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186064894 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399363 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064894 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064894 1071 "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x =-1$?\n$f(x) = {x^{3} + x,\\begin{vmatrix}x < - 1 \\\\ x^{2} + 1,\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge - 1$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186064894 1071 "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x=-1?$ \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+x, & x<-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+1, & x\\ge -1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064894 1074 "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $f(-1)=(-1)^{2}+1=2,$ so $f(-1)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}({x}^{3}+x) \\\\  & =(-1)^{3}+(-1) \\\\  & =-2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the one-sided limits exist but are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-1$ because ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x).$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064896 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063402 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186064896 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399369 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064896 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064896 1071 "![](images/23/237c36808ded541ed264826866db3d2774dc2d5212f1292f21a6c25b3b07d0e1.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y = f(x)$, defined on $[- 3, 5]$. Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave down." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186064896 1071 "![](images/23/237c36808ded541ed264826866db3d2774dc2d5212f1292f21a6c25b3b07d0e1.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=f(x),$ defined on $[-3,5].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave down." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064896 1074 "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nWe can see that the function is curved downward like a frown ($⌢$) over the interval $(1,3).$\n\n![](images/a7/a7d9436275984c12271f2c737eb005e1eaab5b308c2204040e7aff5e87faffb3.png)" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186078500 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399503 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083246 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399617 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087668 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399403 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087836 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399575 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087863 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399396 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087880 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399469 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186088229 1068 #edb/ref 17592186085998 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186088229 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399451 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186088229 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186088229 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} + 4\\frac{dy}{dx} - 12y = 12x^{2} - 32x - 30$\nhas the complementary function $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{2x} + Be^{-6x}$, find the particular solution $y_{p}(x)$ to this equation." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186088229 1071 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-12y=12{x}^{2}-32x-30\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-6x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186088229 1074 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +4(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-12(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=12{x}^{2}-32x-30\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n-12\\alpha {x}^{2}+(8\\alpha -12\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +4\\beta -12\\gamma )=12{x}^{2}-32x-30\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-12\\alpha =12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 8\\alpha -12\\beta =-32\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +4\\beta -12\\gamma =-30\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\beta =2,\\gamma =3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}+2x+3.\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186088232 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086016 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186088232 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399463 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186088232 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186088232 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} - 2\\frac{dy}{dx} + y = 10e^{x}$\nhas the complementary function $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{x} + Bxe^{x}$, find the particular solution $y_{p}(x)$ to this equation." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186088232 1071 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=10{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186088232 1074 "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x},$ we have that $\\lambda =1$ is the double root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $10{e}^{x}.$  In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $1$, which coincides with the double root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ({x}^{2}+2x){e}^{x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha ({x}^{2}+4x+2){e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha ({x}^{2}+4x+2){e}^{x}-2(\\alpha ({x}^{2}+2x){e}^{x})+(\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{x}) & =10{e}^{x} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+4x+2-2{x}^{2}-4x+{x}^{2})\\alpha {e}^{x} & =10{e}^{x} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{x} & =10{e}^{x} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =10 \\\\ \\alpha  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5{x}^{2}{e}^{x}.\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186110657 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399389 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186110664 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399382 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186111362 1068 #edb/ref 17592186109867 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186111362 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399538 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186111362 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186111362 1071 "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y = \\arcsin x$?\n\n1. $(\\frac{π}{6}, \\frac{1}{2})$\n2. $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\frac{π}{3})$\n3. $(0, 2π)$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186111362 1071 "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$\n\nI. $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{1}{2})$\nII. $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$\nIII. $(0,2\\pi )$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186111362 1074 "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ meaning $a=\\operatorname{sin}⁡b.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{1}{2}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{1}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(0,2\\pi ).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(2\\pi ,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=2\\pi $ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,2\\pi )$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186111367 1068 #edb/ref 17592186109897 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186111367 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399550 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186111367 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186111367 1071 "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y = \\arccos x$?\n\n1. $(\\frac{π}{3}, \\frac{1}{2})$\n2. $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\frac{7π}{4})$\n3. $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\frac{3π}{4})$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186111367 1071 "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$\n\nI. $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{1}{2})$\nII. $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{4})$\nIII. $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186111367 1074 "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{1}{2}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{1}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{7\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ does **not** lie within this range.   $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{3\\pi }{4},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186111371 1068 #edb/ref 17592186109921 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186111371 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399556 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186111371 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186111371 1071 "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y = \\arccos x$?\n\n1. $(\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{π}{4})$\n2. $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\frac{π}{4})$\n3. $(1, 0)$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186111371 1071 "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$\n\nI. $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\nII. $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\nIII. $(1,0)$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186111371 1074 "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points.  \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{1}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(1,0).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(0,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=0$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186111372 1068 #edb/ref 17592186109927 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186111372 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399562 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186111372 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186111372 1071 "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y = \\arctan x$?\n\n1. $(1, \\frac{π}{6})$\n2. $(\\sqrt{3}, \\frac{π}{3})$\n3. $(-1,-\\frac{π}{4})$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186111372 1071 "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\nI. $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{6})$\nII. $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$\nIII. $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186111372 1074 "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First,  we consider the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},1)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{4},-1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186115228 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399568 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124454 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399531 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124455 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399524 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124478 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399582 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124478 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399589 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124478 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399596 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124568 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399510 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186138484 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399410 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186147404 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399437 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186147520 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399476 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186147598 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399444 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186148130 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146504 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186148130 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399457 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186148130 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186148130 1071 "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+4{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $P(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+8$\nII. $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-2$\nIII. $2x+7$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186148130 1071 "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$P(x, y)dx + 4y^{3}dy = 0$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $P(x, y)$?\n\n1. $\\cos (x) + 8$\n2. $\\ln (x + 1) - 2$\n3. $2x + 7$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186148130 1074 "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=4{y}^{3}.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{y}^{3})=0\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+8.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+8)=0=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+8$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-3)=0=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-3$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)=2x+7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+7)=0=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=2x+7$ gives an exact equation." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186148153 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146642 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186148153 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399483 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186148153 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186148153 1071 "Find the inverse Laplace transform for $F(s) = \\frac{6}{s^{2} - 6s}$." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186148153 1071 "Find the inverse Laplace transform for $F(s)=\\frac{6}{{s}^{2}-6s}.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186148153 1074 "First, we write $F(s)$ as a sum of partial fractions, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{{s}^{2}-6s}=\\frac{6}{s(s-6)}=\\frac{1}{s-6}-\\frac{1}{s}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{6t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-6},\\,s>6 \\\\ L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the addition property of the Laplace transform, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-6}-\\frac{1}{s}} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-6}}-{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}} \\\\  & ={e}^{6t}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the inverse Laplace transform of $F(s)=\\frac{6}{{s}^{2}-6s}$ is $f(t)={e}^{6t}-1.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152287 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399517 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184198 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399417 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184198 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399424 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184821 1068 #edb/ref 17592186183505 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186184821 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399431 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184821 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184821 1071 "The equation $2x^{2} - 4x - 9 = 0$ can be written in the form $2(x + p)^{2} = q$. Find $q$, and solve the equation." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186184821 1071 "The equation $2{x}^{2}-4x-9=0$ can be written in the form $2(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184821 1074 "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-4x-9 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-4x & =9 \\\\ 2({x}^{2}-2x) & =9 \\\\ 2[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\cdot x+{(1)}^{2}-{(1)}^{2}] & =9 \\\\ 2[{(x-1)}^{2}-1] & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x-1)}^{2}-2\\cdot 1 & =9 \\\\ 2{(x-1)}^{2}-2 & =9 \\\\ 2{(x-1)}^{2} & =9+2 \\\\ 2{(x-1)}^{2} & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $q=11.$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x-1)}^{2} & =11 \\\\ {(x-1)}^{2} & =\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x-1)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}} \\\\ |x-1| & =\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198252 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399356 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399357 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1069 "q-49054" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1071 "![](images/47/47849c87c3c8cf9008b6531e740c6fc57058905cf17e31e4c93c479f337f0fbd.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y={e}^{x}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ that is closest to the point $(1,0)$ satisfies the equation\n\n$$\n2x-2+=0.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1072 #uuid "eb0ef12c-9b61-582b-acd5-7493f7def056" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1074 "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(1,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y={e}^{x}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{({e}^{x}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-2+{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ 2x-2+{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399358 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399359 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399360 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399361 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399362 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399360 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1080 #uuid "606ee288-277b-5ad8-a08e-806ce45db66b" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399358 1081 "{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399358 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399358 1084 #uuid "30d03c60-3151-59b6-b7b9-fd72aa928ebd" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399359 1081 "{e}^{x}{sin}^{2}⁡x" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399359 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399359 1084 #uuid "5f900a01-8dad-54ab-bb5e-4a84ad557187" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399360 1081 "{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399360 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399360 1084 #uuid "a782534b-97b6-5072-b2d6-441797eca761" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399361 1081 "{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399361 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399361 108